{# narrative_section - prose over statement tokens (design 11.2).

   The model receives entity ids and their statement tokens ONLY - never the
   figures, never the statement text. A claim it cannot see it cannot
   paraphrase, so every number on the page arrives by token substitution
   from a STATEMENT entity (L6 catches a paraphrase, L11 an untraceable
   number; a failing section regenerates once and then renders as a
   statement list, fail closed). Prose is worded here; nothing is decided. #}
You are writing one section of a consulting deliverable. Every claim the section may make is listed below as a token. You write the connecting prose; the engine substitutes each token with the exact canonical sentence it stands for. You do not know, and must not guess, what any token says beyond its label.

SECTION
title: {{ section_title }}
purpose: {{ section_purpose }}
audience: {{ audience }}

CLAIMS AVAILABLE (entity id -> token -> label). Use the token verbatim, on its own line or inside a sentence; it will be replaced with the full claim.
{% for c in claims %}
- {{ c.entity_id }} -> {{ c.token }} : {{ c.label }}
{% endfor %}

{% if findings %}
PREVIOUS ATTEMPT WAS REJECTED for these reasons; do not repeat them:
{% for f in findings %}
- {{ f }}
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}
RULES
- Reference claims by token only. Write no number, amount, percentage, date, count, name of a person or name of a system anywhere in the prose; those exist only inside tokens.
- Use each token at most once. Do not reword, split, abbreviate or explain what a token stands for - the token is the whole claim.
- Do not add a claim the tokens do not cover. Connecting prose says how the claims relate (therefore, however, because) and nothing that would itself need evidence.
- When the token list is empty, return the single sentence: "No claims are available for this section." and nothing else.
- Plain prose paragraphs, no headings, no bullet lists, no markdown.

Return ONLY the prose of the section.
